# Chemical Kinetics: The Study of Reaction Rates

> Thermodynamics tells us whether a reaction can happen; kinetics tells
> us how fast it will. The rusting of iron and the explosion of
> gunpowder are both spontaneous — one takes years, the other
> microseconds. Chemical kinetics explains that gap, and gives chemists
> the levers to speed reactions up or slow them down.

## 1. Rate of a Reaction

[definition]
The **rate of a reaction** is the change in concentration of a reactant
or product per unit time. It is always positive, expressed in
$\text{mol L}^{-1}\text{s}^{-1}$, and usually decreases as reactants
deplete.
[/definition]

For a reaction $aA + bB \to cC + dD$, the rate may be tracked through
any species:

@math label="eq:rate-convention"
\text{rate} = -\frac{1}{a}\frac{d[A]}{dt}
= -\frac{1}{b}\frac{d[B]}{dt}
= \frac{1}{c}\frac{d[C]}{dt}
@/math

The negative sign simply converts the *fall* in reactant concentration
into a positive rate. Dividing by stoichiometric coefficients makes the
rate the same whichever species the experiment monitors.

## 2. Factors That Change the Rate

| Factor | Effect | Molecular reason |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Concentration | Rate rises | More collisions per second |
| Temperature | Rate rises steeply | Larger fraction of molecules beats the energy barrier |
| Catalyst | Rate rises | New low-energy pathway |
| Surface area (heterogeneous) | Rate rises | More exposed reactive sites |
| Inhibitor | Rate falls | Blocks catalyst or reactive sites |

A rule of thumb: a $10\ \text{K}$ rise in temperature roughly doubles
or triples the rate of an ordinary reaction — a far stronger effect
than concentration changes produce.

## 3. Rate Law, Order, and Molecularity

Experimentally, the rate of $aA + bB \to$ products often follows:

@math label="eq:rate-law"
\text{rate} = k[A]^x[B]^y
@/math

The exponents $x$ and $y$ are found **only by experiment** — never
from the balanced equation. The sum $x + y$ is the **overall order**,
and $k$ is the rate constant, whose units depend on the order.

| Order | Units of $k$ | Half-life behaviour |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Zero | $\text{mol L}^{-1}\text{s}^{-1}$ | Decreases with concentration |
| First | $\text{s}^{-1}$ | Constant, independent of $[A]$ |
| Second | $\text{L mol}^{-1}\text{s}^{-1}$ | Increases as concentration falls |

[important]
Do not confuse **order** (an experimental quantity, can be zero or
fractional) with **molecularity** (the number of species colliding in
an elementary step — always a whole number, 1, 2, or 3). For complex
reactions the slowest elementary step, the **rate-determining step**,
controls the observed rate law.
[/important]

## 4. Integrated Rate Equations and Half-Life

For a **first-order** reaction ($2A \to$ products style decay,
radioactive-style), the concentration falls exponentially:

@math label="eq:first-order"
k = \frac{2.303}{t}\log\frac{[A]_0}{[A]}
@/math

The **half-life** — time for concentration to halve — is the signature
of first-order kinetics:

@math label="eq:half-life"
t_{1/2} = \frac{0.693}{k}
@/math

**Worked example.** A first-order reaction is 25 percent complete in
40 minutes. When is it 50 percent complete?

Since 25 percent completion means $[A] = 0.75[A]_0$:

@math label="eq:kinetics-example"
k = \frac{2.303}{40}\log\frac{1}{0.75}
= \frac{2.303}{40}(0.125) \approx 7.2 \times 10^{-3}\ \text{min}^{-1}
@/math

@math label="eq:kinetics-thalf"
t_{1/2} = \frac{0.693}{7.2 \times 10^{-3}} \approx 96\ \text{min}
@/math

A faster route: 75 percent left after 40 min means half-life is
$40 \times \log_{0.75} 0.5 \approx 96$ min — the hallmark exponential
decline.

## 5. Collision Theory and Activation Energy

Reactions occur when molecules collide with energy at least equal to
the **activation energy** $E_a$ and with the proper orientation.
Increasing temperature swells the fraction of molecules past this
barrier — the exponential tail of the Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution —
which is why rates climb so steeply with heat.

The **Arrhenius equation** ties the rate constant to temperature:

@math label="eq:arrhenius"
k = A e^{-E_a/RT}
@/math

Here $A$ is the frequency factor. Taking logarithms of Arrhenius'
equation at two temperatures lets exam problems find $E_a$ from a
straight-line plot of $\log k$ against $\frac{1}{T}$ (slope
$= \frac{-E_a}{2.303R}$).

## 6. Catalysts and Catalysis

[definition]
A **catalyst** increases the rate of a reaction by providing an
alternate pathway of lower activation energy, while remaining
chemically unchanged at the end. It accelerates forward and reverse
reactions equally and never shifts the position of equilibrium.
[/definition]

- **Homogeneous catalysis:** catalyst and reactants in one phase —
  acid catalysis of ester hydrolysis, $NO$ in the oxidation of
  $SO_2$ (lead chamber process).
- **Heterogeneous catalysis:** catalyst as a separate phase — iron in
  Haber's ammonia synthesis, platinum in catalytic converters,
  nickel in hydrogenation of oils.
- **Enzymes:** biological catalysts of staggering specificity and
  efficiency, operating near body temperature.

[info title="Exam corner"]
Remember the three things a catalyst never changes: it does not change
$\Delta H$, it does not change the equilibrium constant, and it is not
consumed. It only lowers the hill between reactants and products —
arriving at the same destination, sooner.
[/info]

## 7. Reaction Intermediates in Complex Reactions

Many reactions proceed through elementary steps, e.g. the
iodide-catalysed decomposition of hydrogen peroxide or the
chlorination of methane in light. Species formed in one step and
consumed in a later step — **intermediates** — never appear in the
overall equation or in the final rate law. The step through the
highest-energy transition state (not the intermediate!) is the
rate-determining step. Distinguishing intermediate from
transition state is a classic trap: an intermediate exists for a
finite time at an energy valley; a transition state is the
fleeting hilltop.

[summary]
Kinetics measures speed. Rate laws $\text{rate} = k[A]^x[B]^y$ are
experimental; order is the sum of exponents; molecularity belongs to
elementary steps. First-order reactions obey
$k = \frac{2.303}{t}\log\frac{[A]_0}{[A]}$ with a constant half-life
$t_{1/2} = \frac{0.693}{k}$, while zero-order and second-order
kinetics have their own integrated forms. Temperature acts through the
Arrhenius relation $k = Ae^{-E_a/RT}$ — a $10\ \text{K}$ rise can
double the rate — and catalysts work by cutting $E_a$ without touching
$\Delta H$ or the equilibrium constant. Master rate laws, half-lives,
and Arrhenius plots and every exam question on speed reduces to these
three tools.
[/summary]
